        ":question/problem": "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-2).$",
