      "question": {
        ":question/id": "3bdc99cf-7f58-58bc-8d19-759a3c38b4ba",
        ":question/problem": "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},-4\\sqrt{2}).$",
